ation tests and then deploy that  bias a safe chef or whatever you would  like to use and be able to deploy that  onto virtual machines you can integrate  all that with say a cloud native  environment configuration tool like  deployment manager or an open-source  equivalent something like terraform  where you've got terraform or deployment  manager managing the actual resources  and something like chef and puppet  managing the configuration of your  applications on those resources if you  want to use containers then certainly  things like you know kubernetes which is  open source I mean it was created at  Google but it is definitely open source  is a great container management  orchestration tool seems to be fairly  popular these days and so if you are  using containers then you want to have a  container orchestration tool also  managing that a second question how to  encourage a culture of automation to  accelerate migration that's actually a  fantastic question  I actually firmly believe that  automation is one of the best ways to  accelerate migration and so this is  really important I think one of the  things to do is that to sell IT and  development on the idea that automation  doesn't just help migration it helps  developers no matter where they're  deploying to it helps IT it helps  operations  it helps security because you can  basically take all these steps that are  potentially manual at this point and  codify them you can put them into code  and commit them you can audit them you  can look at them you can see what the  processes are what the procedures are to  be able to improve them to be able to  see what is actually done and you're  taking all this operational overhead off  of people who are doing this manually  and so there are huge benefits well  beyond migration and one of the other  nice things about automation is actually  it's not an all-or-nothing proposition  you don't go from a completely manual to  manually deployed pipeline to a fully  automated one in one step there's baby  steps you can take all along the way and  these incremental baby steps have huge  benefits at every single step if all you  want to do is move to say automated  configuration management with say  ansible then do that and you will see  huge benefits even if you're not fully  automated down further up the pipeline  you're still going to get a lot of  benefit or from the other side if you  just want to implement Jenkins so that  every time you commit to your repository  you're running some tests that has huge  benefits right there already and so I  think the two-pronged approach towards  going to a culture of automation is one  everyone needs to understand this is not  just for migration it benefits  day-to-day operations of all of IT and  to baby steps are hugely beneficial you  don't have to do all or nothing to be  able to really benefit from automation  that's a third question what is the best  way to  get started with the migration I think  you've got to have a reason so why do  you want to move if you're in a data  center are you capacity constrained are  you not happy with your current  equipment is upgrading the equipment  going to be too expensive are you at an  end of a lease is there a reason why  wherever you are currently deployed is  not working well because that's often a  great motivating factor on the other  hand is there something in particular  about moving to Google cloud that is  particularly enticing is there something  very interesting it's about say machine  learning or using kubernetes on Google  cloud that is particularly enticing so I  think getting a good reason for why you  want to migrate is absolutely important  and so once you have that reason this is  the thing that kind of overcomes those  business challenges that I was talking  about earlier about motivating people  and getting people convinced that this  is the best thing to do for your company  is the best way to get started so really  finding that